Algorithmic Analysis — example 1
int ____________ (vector&lt;int&gt; arr, int q) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.size(); i++)
        if (arr[i] == q)
            return i;
    return -1;
}
What questions do you have about the code?

Where we stopped
int idxFirst (vector&lt;int&gt; arr, int q) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.size(); i++)
        if (arr[i] == q)
            return i;
    return -1;
}
How long does it take?

Thirty seconds. They named this function on Friday and got as far as the question. Do not re-open the naming discussion – point at it and move.

Algorithmic analysis — running time
int idxFirst (vector&lt;int&gt; arr, int q) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.size(); i++)
        if (arr[i] == q)
            return i;
    return -1;
}
How long does it take? It depends! 
What does it depend on?






Blanks are for the pen. What the room should land on:

the size of the array
where q is – or whether it is there at all
the speed of the machine

The third one is the one to kill: we are not benchmarking, so machine speed has to go. That is what forces us to count operations instead of seconds – and the first two are what force us into cases.

Best, worst, average
int idxFirst (vector&lt;int&gt; arr, int q) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.size(); i++)
        if (arr[i] == q)
            return i;
    return -1;
}
For an array of size \(n\):

best case
worst case
average case


Best: 1 – q is at index 0. Worst: n – q is last, or absent. Average: about n/2, and it is worth asking average over what? – you have to assume a distribution before “average” means anything. That assumption is where most average-case analysis quietly goes wrong.
Which of the three do we usually report, and why? Worst case: it is a promise you can actually make.

Examining the details
int idxFirst (vector&lt;int&gt; arr, int q) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.size(); i++)
        if (arr[i] == q)
            return i;
    return -1;
}
How many lines are executed in the worst case?
\(T(n) =\)


Does the ___ matter?
Does the ___ matter?

Count it with them, line by line, with the pen. A defensible answer is \(T(n) = 2n + 2\) – the loop test and the comparison each run about n times, plus the initialisation and the final return.
Do not fight about the exact constant. Getting two different numbers from two students is the setup for the next question, not a problem.

Back to where we started
int idxFirst (vector&lt;int&gt; arr, int q) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.size(); i++)
        if (arr[i] == q)
            return i;
    return -1;
}
In the worst case, \(T(n) =\)


Which should we use to describe it?   \(o \quad O \quad \omega \quad \Omega \quad \theta \quad \Theta\)

Worst case is \(\Theta(n)\) – and the interesting part is why not O(n). O is an upper bound and is true but weak: \(O(n^2)\) is also a correct statement about this function. Theta is the one that says what you actually mean.
This is the distinction that gets tested, and it is the one students most reliably fudge.

Intro to algorithm correctness
int ____________ (vector&lt;int&gt;& C, int a) {
    int retloc = a;
    for (int i = a+1; i &lt; C.size(); i++)
        if (C[i] &lt; C[retloc]) {
            retloc = i;
        }
    return retloc;
}
Good name — what does it do?
Running time?
Does it work?

Same three questions as lecture 2, now in one slide, because they know how to do the first two. Move fast on name and runtime – the whole lecture is the third question.
Name: it returns an index, not a value, so idxOfMin in the house style. Careful – lecture 3’s findMin returned the min value. Worth naming the difference out loud; it is the same distinction they fought over in lecture 2.
Runtime: \(\Theta(n)\), one pass, no early return.
“Does it work?” is the new question. Nobody has an answer yet, which is the point.

What are we trying to prove?
int idxOfMin (vector&lt;int&gt;& C, int a) {
    int retloc = a;
    for (int i = a+1; i &lt; C.size(); i++)
        if (C[i] &lt; C[retloc]) {
            retloc = i;
        }
    return retloc;
}
Thm. idxOfMin(C, a) returns the index of the minimum value in C[a..n-1].

The theorem comes first, deliberately. You cannot choose an invariant until you know what you are trying to deliver – the invariant is reverse-engineered from the theorem, and doing it in this order is what makes the next slide feel inevitable rather than arbitrary.
Ask: at the moment the loop exits, what would we need to be true?

Initialization and restoration
int idxOfMin (vector&lt;int&gt;& C, int a) {
    int retloc = a;
    for (int i = a+1; i &lt; C.size(); i++)
        if (C[i] &lt; C[retloc]) {
            retloc = i;
        }
    return retloc;
}
Initialization. \(i = a{+}1\):
Restoration. Assume invariant holds at the top of \(i\), and then run the body:

Initialization: retloc = a, and the min of the one-element range C[a..a] is at index \(a\). Holds.
Maintenance: the body compares C[i] with C[retloc] and moves retloc only if C[i] is strictly smaller. Either way retloc ends up the index of the min of C[a..i] – which is the invariant at the top of \(i{+}1\). Done.
Worth naming: the invariant is false in the middle of the body, between the comparison and the assignment. It only has to hold at the top of each iteration. Students who think it must hold everywhere get stuck here.

Termination
int idxOfMin (vector&lt;int&gt;& C, int a) {
    int retloc = a;
    for (int i = a+1; i &lt; C.size(); i++)
        if (C[i] &lt; C[retloc]) {
            retloc = i;
        }
    return retloc;
}
Does the loop stop?
What is the state when it does?

Stops: \(i\) rises by one each pass against a fixed bound, so after \(n-a-1\) passes the guard fails.
The second question is the one that matters and the one nobody writes down. Two facts combine:

the guard is false, so \(i = n\)
the invariant holds, so retloc indexes the min of C[a..i-1]

Substitute: C[a..n-1]. That is the theorem, and the proof ends – not with more argument, but by reading the invariant at exit.
If the invariant had been a..i, this substitution would have given C[a..n], which is out of bounds. The off-by-one from two slides ago shows up here as a proof that does not close.

The function
The same one from lecture 1, name still blank:
int ____________ (vector&lt;int&gt; arr, int q) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.size(); i++)
        if (arr[i] == q)
            return i;
    return -1;
}
It is deliberately trivial. Nobody can hide behind complexity, so the only thing that can fail is the precision of their words.
A description that survives everything below:

Given a vector of integers arr and an integer q, return the index of the first element of arr equal to q, scanning from index 0 upward. If no element equals q, return -1.

The code
void selectionSort (vector&lt;int&gt;& C) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; C.size(); i++) {
        int m = idxOfMin(C, i);
        swap(C, m, i);
    }
}
Write \(n\) for C.size(), and \(C_0\) for the contents of C on entry. Note \(n\) does not change: nothing in the loop resizes C.

