Where we left off
int idxOfMin (vector&lt;int&gt;& C, int a) {
    int retloc = a;
    for (int i = a+1; i &lt; C.size(); i++)
        if (C[i] &lt; C[retloc]) {
            retloc = i;
        }
    return retloc;
}
We named it, evaluated its runtime, and proved it is correct.

Thirty seconds of recap – this is the function we proved on Wednesday. \(\Theta(n)\), and the invariant was “retloc is the index of the min of C[a..i-1]”.
We are about to use it as a building block, which is the reason it was worth proving.

Termination
void selectionSort (vector&lt;int&gt;& C) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; C.size(); i++) {
        int m = idxOfMin(C, i);
        swap(C, m, i);
    }
}
Does the loop stop?
What is the state when it does?

Stops: \(i\) rises by one against a fixed bound, so after \(n\) iterations the guard fails.
At exit \(i = n\), so the invariant reads: C[0..n-1] is sorted and holds the \(n\) smallest values – which is the whole array. Sorted. That is the theorem.
Note the second clause does nothing at all at this point. It existed only to get us here, and then it retires.

